ne outcome schema |\n127\t| IL5-Q5 | the milestone that owns verbalization |\n128\t| IL5-Q6 | the milestone that owns `gnd.agents` |\n129\t| IL4-Q3 (remainder), IL-close-Q1, the `hold()` import | the next milestone that changes the mined import: one public counting entry, one rendering pass |\n130\t\n131\t## Close review (part D)\n132\t\n133\tPending: the maintained panel over the plan (spec), this record (results) and\n134\tthe close plan (delta).\n135\t\n136\t## Boundary -- what the milestone does NOT establish\n137\t\n138\t- **Which learner is better.** Each row is one run on one small example.\n139\t- **Reproducibility.** No run was repeated to measure it.\n140\t- **Any learner in CI.** Tests replay committed outputs; nothing installs or\n141\t  runs a learner.\n142\t- **That an own-reference subtype's joins are counted.** They are refused,\n143\t  never admitted (`008`, ILC-F8).\n144\t- **Acceptance.** The owner's acceptance is a separate record."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_BBwgRgwEI6D5r52gX9LXnDJd","content":"1\t# ILP learners\n2\t\n3\tr3 (p2), 2026-10-27. **Approved by the owner on 2026-10-27, as recorded in `001-plan-gate.md`.** Plan review closed by its stop rule.\n4\tClose repairs (c), 2026-10-29: wording that records what happened, applied at the owner's\n5\tdirection without review (plan §8, p2; close plan Part B). None changes an approved criterion's intent.\n6\tMilestone: `ilp-learners`. `auto=false`.\n7\tInspection baseline: `63f7f1f` (main, after PR #45, the induced-knowledge close).\n8\t\n9\t**Review status (p1):**\n10\t- **Round 1.** r1 (SHA-256 `6cb10d97…e463`) received four reader opinions:\n11\t  all four request-changes, 47 findings, 14 blocking, all reproduced. See the\n12\t  [round-1 disposition](plan-review-r1-disposition.md).\n13\t- **The owner's rulings,** on 2026-10-27 (o): the owner approved the\n14\t  disposition's repairs and its proposed answers for D2 and D8–D12.\n15\t- **r2** applied them.\n16\t- **Round 2 (confirmation).** r2 (SHA-256 `e0668e30…3bb9`) received four\n17\t  approve-with-fixes opinions. 13 of the 14 round-1 blockers were resolved and\n18\t  R4-F7 partially; there were 18 new non-blocking findings (12 distinct), all\n19\t  introduced by r2. The self-inflicted ratio was 1.0, so review stopped. See\n20\t  the [round-2 disposition](plan-review-r2-disposition.md) (p2).\n21\t- **r3 (p2).** The owner directed, 2026-10-27 (o): \"Apply themes A–I as r3\n22\t  with no further review round, D13 yes.\" r3 records those repairs and D13 and\n23\t  nothing else. **r3 bytes carry no review opinion.**\n24\t- **Approval (c).** The owner approved r3 and the IL1 spec r3 on 2026-10-27;\n25\t  the approval is recorded in `001-plan-gate.md`, not here.\n26\t\n27\t**Change tags:**\n28\t- `(p1)` marks text added or changed in r2 in response to round 1.\n29\t- `(p2)` marks text added or changed in r3 in response to round 2.\n30\t- `(o)` marks an owner answer or ruling, quoted where the owner's words exist.\n31\t- `(c)` marks a close repair, 2026-10-29 (close plan Part B); it carries no review opinion.\n32\t- Untagged text is the author's r1 proposal.\n33\t- Planning authorship is not approval.\n34\t- The retained snapshot under `evidence/plan-review-r1/` is the authoritative\n35\t  record of r1's bytes.\n36\t\n37\t## Goal, status and completion predicates\n38\t\n39\tThe induced-knowledge milestone (IK) built a tool-neutral ILP bridge (its D4).\n40\tIt connected one learner, Popper, without ever running it (IK5-D13 (a)), and it\n41\tsurveyed five other learner families for later adapters. This milestone:\n42\t- connects four of those families to the same bridge;\n43\t- runs every learner, Popper included, once for real, recorded as evidence.\n44\t\n45\tThe four families:\n46\t- **ILASP / FastLAS** (answer set programming);\n47\t- **Boolean rule sets** (a sequential-covering learner, RIPPER, IL3-D1 (a); integer-programming\n48\t  and certifiable learners were not integrated) (c);\n49\t- **an AMIE-style exact rule miner** (unlabelled Horn-rule mining);\n50\t- **an LLM proposer** (a language model writes clauses).\n51\t\n52\t**What does not change: the bridge's trust model.**\n53\t- A learner proposes.\n54\t- GND re-checks every learned rule over the same inputs, computes its own\n55\t  coverage and support, and refuses any disagreement.\n56\t- Survivors are admitted only as `induced`/`proposed`/`deontic` constraints,\n57\t  through `gnd evolve` (IK-R1, IK-R2).\n58\t- Round 1 confirmed this admission gate holds (reader 1).\n59\t\n60\tPlanning only, as written. Executed since (c): every phase is recorded,\n61\t`002` to `007`, and the close in `008` and `009`; this plan claims none of\n62\ttheir results.\n63\t\n64\tCompletion requires:\n65\t\n66\t1. **IL1:**\n67\t   - **One learner registry** that every adapter enters. Popper and `neutral`\n68\t     are its first entries, in that declared order.\n69\t   - **Compatibility:** every IK5, IK6 and RF1 golden stays byte-identical,\n70\t     except where D12's join check refuses a clause (p1).\n71\t   - **The type-confused join check** refuses, for every target, a clause\n72\t     that joins values of different object types (D12 (o)) (p1), or whose\n73\t     comparison's declared type differs from its operand's (D13 (o)) (p2).\n74\t   - **SWI octal escapes** are read (IK5-Q2).\n75\t   - **One real Popper run** over the IK6 refunds export records one\n76\t     termination value and one import value, from lists closed before the run\n77\t     (p2). A default-gate replay test re-imports its committed output and\n78\t     asserts the recorded exit (p1).\n79\t2. **IL2:**\n80\t   - `--target ilasp` (and `fastlas`, if IL2-D1 keeps it) renders an ASP\n81\t     learning task from the neutral form. The task is golden across two\n82\t     `PYTHONHASHSEED` values.\n83\t   - Learned ASP rules are parsed into the existing clause form, where the\n84\t     shared fragment checker accepts them or refuses them by name.\n85\t   - One real run is recorded and replayed (p1).\n86\t3. **IL3:**\n87\t   - **The export:** a propositional feature export of **thresholded**\n88\t     features only (p1), golden across two hash seeds.\n89\t   - **The import:** a learned rule set's positive single-feature conjuncts map\n90\t     to clauses. Every other shape is refused by name: multi-feature\n91\t     conjuncts, negated features (p1) and ordered lists. Admission stays\n92\t     all-or-nothing (D10 (o)) (p1).\n93\t   - One real run is recorded and replayed.\n94\t4. **IL4:**\n95\t   - **The export:** an unlabelled export of a population as typed binary\n96\t     relations (p1).\n97\t   - **The import:** mined Horn rules become `guarded` constraints.\n98\t     - GND counts support and confidence over distinct head pairs (p1).\n99\t     - A rule whose GND support differs from the miner-reported count refuses\n100\t       `support-mismatch`.\n101\t     - Only rules that hold on the population are admitted (D11 (o)) (p1).\n102\t   - One real run is recorded and replayed.\n103\t5. **IL5:**\n104\t   - An LLM proposer with its own registry entry, `llm` (p1). Its answer is\n105\t     accepted only as `gnd-ilp-clauses/1` JSON, through the shared re-check.\n106\t   - One real run with a named agent and model is recorded, **only after D8 is\n107\t     ruled** at the IL5 gate (p1). If D8 declines the run, IL5 completes with\n108\t     fixture-only evidence and records the outcome `not run (D8)` (p2).\n109\t6. **IL6:** one public anonymized walk runs the labelled adapters on the IK6\n110\t   refunds example, and `amie` on the committed staffing fixture, which has\n111\t   the relations a miner needs (c), and exits as recorded at every step. An adapter whose run is\n112\t   `not run (D8)` shows that outcome (p2).\n113\t   - **The comparison table** is committed, with the columns fixed in §4\n114\t     (p1). AMIE has its own unlabelled row (p1).\n115\t   - **Docs, skill surfaces and entry points** are updated, and each census row\n116\t     has a check.\n117\t7. **Every phase has:**\n118\t   - approval;\n119\t   - focused checks;\n120\t   - independent diff review;\n121\t   - refactoring review;\n122\t   - a record.\n123\t\n124\t   **Close has:**\n125\t   - four-reviewer evidence;\n126\t   - a question sweep;\n127\t   - a PR;\n128\t   - separately recorded owner acceptance.\n129\t\n130\tNo delivery date is committed.\n131\t\n132\t## 0. Binding design rules\n133\t\n134\tIK-R1 to IK-R12 of the induced-knowledge plan\n135\t(`../induced-knowledge/notes/induced-knowledge-plan.md` §0) bind every phase\n136\tunchanged. This milestone adds:\n137\t\n138\t| ID | Rule |\n139\t|---|---|\n140\t| IL-R1 | **No learner is a dependency.** No learner, solver, JVM, Prolog, ASP system, Python rule-learning package or model SDK enters `pyproject.toml`, the built wheel's `Requires-Dist` (p1), or a required lane. GND never imports one. |\n141\t| IL-R2 | **Live runs are scratch evidence.** A learner runs only in a scratch environment outside the repository, through `gnd.process.run_bounded_process`, from an evidence script under `evidence/IL<n>/`. A live test carries the existing `live` marker and is never selected by the default gate. **Installs are confined to a scratch prefix: no system package manager, no `sudo`, no global PATH or shell-profile edits; a prebuilt binary or jar is a download, approved at its phase gate with URL, filename, size and SHA-256 (D2 (o)) (p1).** |\n142\t| IL-R3 | **One registry, one fragment, one re-check.** Every adapter is one registry entry. Every labelled adapter's output reaches the one clause form and the one fragment checker (`gnd.export.ilp_rewrite`). IL4's sibling contract is fixed in §2. No adapter copies the re-check or the proposal builder. |\n143\t| IL-R4 | **A learner's numbers are claims.** Coverage, support, confidence and scores reported by a learner are compared with GND's own and never written into provenance (IK-R2). A disagreement refuses with a named reason. **A run record's coverage comes from the learner or from an evaluator that is not GND (for example SWI or clingo over the rendered files, or the learner's own `predict`), named in the record; the script never derives it with GND. The learner's own printed figures are kept verbatim and compared where they exist (p1).** |\n144\t| IL-R5 | **Licences before downloads (p1).** Each phase records the learner's licence from a cited upstream URL, with its date, before any download. A licence that is not a permissive OSI licence, a repository without a licence, or a registration or click-through step stops for an owner ruling. No learner binary or source is committed. |\n145\t| IL-R6 | **Untrusted output, never executed.** Learner and LLM output is parsed by a closed, capped, total reader (standing rule 1). Nothing a learner writes is evaluated as code. Prompt text built from host values is neutralized (IK-R5). |\n146\t| IL-R7 | **IK5 compatibility.** The `gnd-ilp/1`, `gnd-ilp-clauses/1`, `gnd-ilp-run/1`, `gnd-ilp-import/1` and `gnd-ilp-export/1` wires, the Popper rendering and every IK5 and IK6 golden stay byte-identical unless a phase spec names the change. **Adding a target widens the closed `target` enums of `ilp.schema.json` and `ilp-import.schema.json` within `/1` (D9 (o)); every existing document stays valid. A test asserts both enums equal the registry's targets (p1): `ilp.schema.json` equals `EXPORT_TARGETS` and `ilp-import.schema.json` equals `IMPORT_TARGETS`, the targets `gnd import ilp-rules` accepts (p2).** |\n147\t| IL-R8 (p1) | **Synthetic inputs only.** Every live run reads only the IK6 refunds example or a committed synthetic fixture under `tests/fixtures/ilp_learners/`, named in its phase spec, with the export digest recorded. No host data reaches a learner or a provider. |\n148\t| IL-R9 (p1) | **Outcomes before runs.** Before each live run its spec fixes the closed outcome as two fields (p2): `termination` ∈ {`rendering-rejected`, `completed`, `learner-timeout`, `wall-clock-kill`, `output-limit`} and `import` ∈ {`none`, `refused:<name>`, `admitted`}, where an unparsable program is `refused:unparsable`. It also fixes the program-extraction rule as one function shared by the script and the replay test (p2), the learner settings with the full argv, all six `ProcessLimits` fields, and the learner's own timeout below the wall clock, where the learner has one; ILASP 4.4.1 and the agent CLIs have none, so `learner-timeout` is unreachable there and the wall clock is the bound (c). Every invocation is numbered and retained; the first after the gate is the run of record. A second run needs an owner ruling and is reported beside the first. A rendering or settings fix after a run is an owner-recorded amendment naming the changed goldens and the `adapter_version` bump; a parser-only fix that changes no golden stays in scope. Each record states, for every number, whether it came from the learner or the script, plus seeds, threads, and \"single run, reproducibility not measured\". The import is run with fixed `--requested-at`, `--actor` and `--format json`, from a fixed working directory with relative paths, so the replay can reproduce it (p2). |\n149\t| IL-R10 (p1) | **Evidence is private-clean.** A test scans this milestone directory for home paths, usernames and the downstream-name digests, with a negative control. Evidence scripts pass `HOME`, `USER` and `TMPDIR` into the bounded environment so redaction applies where it can, and write install commands with a `<scratch>` placeholder. Because `run_bounded_process` redacts only values of 4 or more bytes and only the child's output, each script also substitutes `<scratch>` and `<home>` itself in every file it writes; the scan is the backstop and matches the username as a whole path component, whatever its length (p2). |\n150\t| IL-R11 (p1) | **All-or-nothing admission** (IK5-D9) holds for every adapter (D10 (o)). |\n151\t\n152\t## 1. Scope, sources and authority\n153\t\n154\t**The request.** The owner, in session on 2026-10-27 (o): \"In the inductive\n155\tlogic addition we found several ilp packages but we integrated only one. I want\n156\ta new milestone that addss more\".\n157\t\n158\t**Adapter selection (o), 2026-10-27.**\n159\t- **The question:** \"Which learner adapters should the new milestone add to\n160\t  the ILP bridge?\"\n161\t- **The owner's selection:** \"ILASP / FastLAS (ASP),Boolean rule sets,AMIE-style\n162\t  miner,LLM proposer\".\n163\t- **Not offered (p1):** neural rule learners, the fifth surveyed family. Leaving\n164\t  them out is this plan's proposal.\n165\t\n166\t**Live runs (o), 2026-10-27.**\n167\t- **The question:** \"Should this milestone run real learners, which means\n168\t  installing them (e.g. SWI-Prolog, clingo, Popper) in a scratch environment?\"\n169\t- **The answer:** \"Yes, one recorded run each\".\n170\t- **The confirmation (p1):** after round 1, the owner confirmed its reach:\n171\t  \"D2 yes (scratch prefix, downloads approved per gate)\" (o). It covers one\n172\t  recorded run per adapter, Popper included, and the JVM, AMIE, ILASP/FastLAS\n173\t  and rule-set installs, within IL-R2's limits.\n174\t- **What it supersedes (p1):** the earlier decline, IK5-D13 (a) (2026-10-26),\n175\t  and the carried row in `../induced-knowledge/012-milestone-close.md` that\n176\t  says \"installing Popper was declined\".\n177\t- **What it does not cover (p1):** the IL5 LLM run. That run uses provider\n178\t  credentials and sends data to a provider, so it needs its own selection\n179\t  (D8).\n180\t\n181\t**The plan review (o), 2026-10-27.** The owner selected four local reviewer\n182\tagents (D5).\n183\t\n184\t**Inherited from the induced-knowledge milestone:**\n185\t- IK plan D4: the bridge, and \"other families surveyed for later adapters\";\n186\t- IK5 spec §6 and §9;\n187\t- record 007;\n188\t- record 012's carried rows;\n189\t- IK plan §7: no learner in CI or a required lane.\n190\t\n191\t### Harvested obligations\n192\t\n193\t| Obligation | Source | Owner |\n194\t|---|---|---|\n195\t| Further adapters \"are later work on the same bridge\" | IK plan §2 request 5; IK5 spec §9 | IL2–IL5 |\n196\t| IK5-Q5: a real Popper run; whether its output fits the IK2 block | 007; 012 carried rows | IL1 |\n197\t| IK5-Q2: SWI octal escapes in Popper text | 007; 012 | IL1 |\n198\t| IK6 join space: the stand-in learner's join branch has no case (p1: restated) | 010:164–166; 012 | IL1 (C5) |\n199\t| IK4Q2-D3: the reference-bearing ILP export | 012 | D7 (proposed out) |\n200\t| `--target` names the adapter; `ilp-<target>` method token | IK5 spec §6; IK2-D11 | IL1 registry |\n201\t| No GND command runs a learner | IK5-D12 (o) | D3 |\n202\t| Recorded fixtures bind the export digest and learner name and version | IK5-C4 | every phase |\n203\t| ILP learners never in CI or a required lane | IK plan §7; IK-R8 | IL-R2 |\n204\t| The neutral form and import contract stated learner-independently | IK5-C1 | IL-R3 |\n205\t| A packaged skill for the induction loop | IK6-Q1 → \"its own milestone\" | out of scope (§7) |\n206\t| (p1) The type-confused join gap in the shipped `neutral` import | round 1, R1-F2 | IL1 (D12) |\n207\t\n208\t## 2. Contract proposal and product behaviour\n209\t\n210\t### The learner registry (IL1)\n211\t\n212\tOne table in a new module, `gnd.export.ilp_learners`, with one entry per\n213\tadapter, **in declaration order: `popper`, then `neutral`, then each later\n214\tadapter appended (p1).**\n215\t\n216\t| Field | Meaning |\n217\t|---|---|\n218\t| `target` | the `--target` spelling |\n219\t| `learner` | the run record's required `learner.name`; for `neutral`, any name that is **not** another registered learner's (p1) |\n220\t| `method` | the IK2 `method` token, `ilp-<target>` |\n221\t| `exports` | whether `gnd export ilp` accepts this target (`neutral` does, writing `neutral.json` only) (p1) |\n222\t| `render` | export input → files; its input type is set by `contract` (p1) |\n223\t| `parse` | learner output bytes → `gnd-ilp-clauses/1`, or `None` for JSON input |\n224\t| `contract` | `labelled-clauses` (IK5's), or `unlabelled-horn` (IL4's) (p1) |\n225\t\n226\t**Derived tuples (p2):**\n227\t- `EXPORT_TARGETS`: the entries with `exports=True`.\n228\t- `IMPORT_TARGETS`: the entries with `contract == \"labelled-clauses\"`, which\n229\t  is exactly what `gnd import ilp-rules` accepts.\n230\t- **Values:** `popper` and `neutral` are in both. `llm` is import-only\n231\t  (`exports=False`). `amie` (`unlabelled-horn`) is export-only for `gnd export\n232\t  ilp` and is imported by `gnd import mined-rules`.\n233\t\n234\t**What changes in `ilp_service.py`:**\n235\t- `LEARNER = {\"popper\": \"popper\"}` and the `if target == \"popper\"` branches\n236\t  become registry lookups.\n237\t- The command modules keep literal `choices=` tuples. A test asserts by parser\n238\t  introspection that they equal the registry. This adds no entry-point edge and\n239\t  no import at parser-construction time (RF5) (p1).\n240\t- Every IK5 golden stays byte-identical (IL-R7).\n241\t\n242\t### The type-confused join check (IL1, D12 (o)) (p1)\n243\t\n244\t- **Where:** a new fragment check, `type-confused-join`, as one shared function\n245\t  in `gnd.export.ilp_rewrite`. Both the `wrong/1` rewriter and IL4's Horn-rule\n246\t  mapping call it (p2).\n247\t- **What it refuses:** a variable whose join sites resolve to object types\n248\t  that are not compatible under the same ancestor-closure predicate that\n249\t  `semantic:op-term-type-compat` uses (`model/_validation.py`\n250\t  `_ancestor_closure`).\n251\t- **Join sites (p2):**\n252\t  - every atom argument position the variable occupies;\n253\t  - a comparison predicate's declared type, which counts as a site of its\n254\t    variable operand (D13 (o));\n255\t  - **not** anonymous `_`: each `_` occurrence is its own fresh variable, so it\n256\t    is excluded.\n257\t- **Type source (p2):** a closure map computed once from ModelIndex, carried on\n258\t  the import's prepared state, never on the `gnd-ilp/1` wire.\n259\t- **Scope:** it runs for every target, `neutral` included.\n260\t- **What it closes:** today a clause joining a refund id with an amount\n261\t  validates with exit 0.\n262\t- **Effect on the shipped bridge:** this narrows `gnd import ilp-rules`. An IK5\n263\t  corpus case that changes exit is listed in the IL1 record as an intended\n264\t  behaviour change.\n265\t\n266\t### ILASP / FastLAS (IL2)\n267\t\n268\t**Export: `--target ilasp` writes `ilasp/task.las`.**\n269\t- **Background:** only the comparison definitions (p1).\n270\t- **Examples:** one context-dependent example per population (`#pos`/`#neg`).\n271\t  Its context holds **that population's facts only, without the example\n272\t  argument** (p1).\n273\t- **Mode bias:**\n274\t  - `#modeh(wrong)`;\n275\t  - one `#modeb` per exported predicate, with arity equal to the predicate's\n276\t    arity minus 1 and typed placeholders;\n277\t  - one `#modeb` per exported comparison (p1).\n278\t\n279\t**Import: a closed ASP rule reader.**\n280\t- **Mapping:** a learned `wrong :- body.` becomes `wrong(X) :- body'`, with X\n281\t  threaded as the example argument. This step exactly inverts the rendering.\n282\t- **Refused by construct name (p1):**\n283\t  - constraints (`:- body.`);\n284\t  - choice rules;\n285\t  - weak constraints;\n286\t  - classical negation (`-p`);\n287\t  - arithmetic terms;\n288\t  - intervals and pools;\n289\t  - directives.\n290\t- **Reaching the fragment checker:** `not`, aggregates and disjunction get\n291\t  there and are refused by name.\n292\t- **Constants:** a fragment-table row fixes how the reader maps symbolic\n293\t  constants, quoted strings and integers (p1).\n294\t\n295\t**Expected finding:** ASP learners often use negation as failure. The refusal\n296\trate is a recorded result, not a defect.\n297\t\n298\t### Boolean rule sets (IL3)\n299\t\n300\t**Export: `gnd export ilp --target rules` writes a propositional feature table,\n301\t`gnd-ilp-features/1`.**\n302\t- One row per population, one column per feature.\n303\t- **A feature** is one exported predicate with one integer threshold, for\n304\t  example `exists f_order_amount(_,A) with A > t`.\n305\t- **Unthresholded features are not exported:** as a single-atom clause they\n306\t  could only refuse `non-ground-absence` (p1).\n307\t- **Thresholds** are the role's observed values, up to a bound fixed in the\n308\t  IL3 spec.\n309\t- **The schema** declares each column's literal meaning (predicate, role,\n310\t  operator, threshold), so a reader cannot reinterpret it (p1).\n311\t\n312\t**Import: polarity table (p1).**\n313\t\n314\t| Learner literal | Maps to |\n315\t|---|---|\n316\t| feature true (`= 1`, `> 0.5` on a binary column) | its clause |\n317\t| feature false or negated (`= 0`, `<= 0.5`) | refused `negation-as-failure` |\n318\t| conjunct of two or more features | refused `propositional-conjunction` |\n319\t| ordered rule list (`else`) | refused `ordered-rule-list` |\n320\t\n321\tAdmission is all-or-nothing (IL-R11). Each learner output spelling has a\n322\ttest.\n323\t\n324\t### AMIE-style miner (IL4)\n325\t\n326\t**Command shape (p1).**\n327\t- **Export:** `gnd export ilp --target amie MODEL --population POP --out DIR`.\n328\t  `--labels` and `--population` are mutually exclusive, and the target decides\n329\t  which one is required.\n330\t  - **Post-parse check (p2):** argparse cannot express the target deciding\n331\t    which one is required, so a post-parse check enforces it, with exit 64.\n332\t  - **Unchanged (p2):** the missing-`--labels` exit for `popper` and `neutral`.\n333\t  - **Moved goldens (p2):** making `--labels` non-required moves two existing\n334\t    RF1 goldens, the `gnd export ilp` help usage line and the `unknown_flag`\n335\t    exit stderr (\"required: …\"). IL4 lists both.\n336\t- **Import:** `gnd import mined-rules MODEL RULES --population POP\n337\t  --reported FILE --target amie --out PROPOSAL`, a new subcommand.\n338\t  - **Home (p2):** it lives in `gnd.commands.checking`, which is already bound\n339\t    to `gnd.export.ilp_service`, so there is no new `gnd_entrypoints.json`\n340\t    binding.\n341\t  - **Goldens:** its RF1 goldens are new files.\n342\t  - **Report:** it has its own report, with `command` value `import\n343\t    mined-rules` and a schema named in the IL4 spec (p2).\n344\t\n345\t**Export contents.**\n346\t- **Relations:** each binary asserted fact type is one relation.\n347\t- **Typing:** each entity is spelled with its object-type prefix, so joins stay\n348\t  typed (p1).\n349\t- **Other arities** are skipped and listed.\n350\t- **No labels:** the miner looks for regularities, not for a `wrong` target.\n351\t\n352\t**Import mapping.**\n353\t- `B1, …, Bn ⇒ r(x, y)` becomes `guarded`: the body is the antecedent and an\n354\t  `atom` consequent is the head.\n355\t- This is a sibling of IK5's contract, not its `wrong/1` rewriting.\n356\t- The shared type-check function and validation apply. IL4 calls it directly\n357\t  and has its own test (p2).\n358\t\n359\t**Counting (p1).**\n360\t- **Numerator:** |{(x,y) : ∃z body ∧ head}|.\n361\t- **Denominator:** |{(x,y) : ∃z body}|.\n362\t- **Confidence:** `confidence.value` = numerator / denominator, with formula\n363\t  `std-confidence-head-pairs`.\n364\t- **The comparison:** the miner's support, its count of distinct head pairs\n365\t  (AMIE prints no positive examples; IL4-F4) (c), must equal GND's\n366\t  numerator; otherwise the rule refuses `support-mismatch`.\n367\t- **Not compared:** PCA confidence. It is kept verbatim as the miner's claim.\n368\t- **Weaker than IK5:** only counts are compared, because the miner emits no\n369\t  sets.\n370\t- **Zero body pairs:** the rule is refused `covers-nothing`.\n371\t\n372\t**Admission (D11 (o)) (p1).**\n373\t- A rule is admitted only if it holds on the population: numerator equals\n374\t  denominator, the same bar as `gnd discover` (IK4-C1).\n375\t- A rule whose head fact type is not in the population's `covers` is refused\n376\t  `open-world-head`.\n377\t- `examples` are opaque fact ids, never raw values (IK-R11). The provenance\n378\t  `manifest_sha256` is the population digest, as `gnd discover` writes it.\n379\t\n380\t### LLM proposer (IL5)\n381\t\n382\t- **Registry entry (p1):** `target: llm`, `parse: None` (JSON input),\n383\t  `method: ilp-llm`, `exports: False` (p2). The learner name must be the agent adapter id, and the\n384\t  model id is recorded as the agent reports it.\n385\t- **The prompt builder** gets a source home in the `export` lane,\n386\t  `gnd.export.ilp_prompt` (p1). It is built from the neutral export, the\n387\t  model's verbalization and object-type descriptions (IK1), neutralized.\n388\t  Host strings are untrusted text (IL-R6).\n389\t- **The runner** is an evidence script that selects an agent through\n390\t  `gnd.agents`, not a GND command (D3).\n391\t- **The run record's coverage** comes from SWI evaluating the clauses over the\n392\t  Popper rendering of the same export, not from GND (IL-R4) (p1).\n393\t- **Not repeatable:** an LLM run cannot be repeated. The recorded output is\n394\t  bound by digest, and the record says so.\n395\t- **Blocked until D8 (p1):** the live run is blocked until the IL5 gate rules\n396\t  the agent, model, credential source, call or cost ceiling, and provider\n397\t  terms.\n398\t- **If D8 declines the run (p2):** IL5 keeps fixture-only evidence, records the\n399\t  outcome `not run (D8)`, and the IL6 row shows it.\n400\t\n401\t## 3. Dependencies and artifact homes\n402\t\n403\tDAG (p1): **IL1 → IL2 → IL5 → IL3 → IL4 → IL6 → close**. The chain follows D4.\n404\tIL2–IL5 are independent in content, but they share the registry, the CLI\n405\tchoices, the schema enums, `ilp-bridge.md` and the RF1 goldens, so they run\n406\tone at a time.\n407\t\n408\t| Artifact | Proposed home |\n409\t|---|---|\n410\t| Plan, specs, records, evidence, live-run scripts | this milestone directory |\n411\t| Registry | `src/gnd/export/ilp_learners.py` (new, `export` lane) |\n412\t| Type-confused join check | `src/gnd/export/ilp_rewrite.py` (p1) |\n413\t| ASP adapter | `src/gnd/export/ilp_asp.py` |\n414\t| Feature export and rule-set import | `src/gnd/export/ilp_rules.py`; schema `src/gnd/ilp-features.schema.json` |\n415\t| Triple export and Horn-rule import | `src/gnd/export/ilp_amie.py`; `gnd import mined-rules` (p1) |\n416\t| LLM prompt builder | `src/gnd/export/ilp_prompt.py` (p1); runner in `evidence/IL5/` |\n417\t| Fixtures | `tests/fixtures/ilp_learners/` (synthetic, anonymized) |\n418\t| Docs | `docs/reference/ilp-bridge.md` (normative home), `cli.md`, `capabilities.md`, release notes |\n419\t\n420\tEach new module is assigned in `tools/gnd_layers.json` before use, and the\n421\tallowance count stays zero (IK-R7). A command in a new command module would be\n422\tbound in `tools/gnd_entrypoints.json` (p1). None is planned:\n423\t`gnd import mined-rules` joins `gnd.commands.checking`, which is already bound\n424\t(p2).\n425\t\n426\t## 4. Phases, criteria and residuals\n427\t\n428\t### IL1 — Learner registry, join check and a real Popper run\n429\t\n430\tDraft spec r3: [phase-IL1-spec.md](phase-IL1-spec.md) (p1, p2).\n431\t\n432\t- **IL1-C1 (p1):** one registry, in declaration order. `ilp_service.py` has no\n433\t  learner-name branch. A fake registry entry, monkeypatched in, reaches\n434\t  `render`, the `method` token and the learner check. The export `choices=`\n435\t  and `ilp.schema.json` enum equal `EXPORT_TARGETS`; the import `choices=` and\n436\t  `ilp-import.schema.json` enum equal `IMPORT_TARGETS` (p2). Every IK5, IK6\n437\t  and RF1 golden is byte-identical, except corpus cases that C2 refuses, which\n438\t  are listed.\n439\t- **IL1-C2 (p1):**\n440\t  - A clause that joins incompatible object types exits 3\n441\t    `type-confused-join`, for `popper` and `neutral`.\n442\t  - A comparison whose declared type is incompatible with its operand's role\n443\t    type exits 3 `type-confused-join` (D13 (o)) (p2).\n444\t  - A clause that joins compatible types is unchanged, and so is a clause with\n445\t    `_` at two incompatible types (p2).\n446\t  - A neutral run record that names a registered learner exits 3\n447\t    `learner-mismatch`.\n448\t- **IL1-C3 (p1):**\n449\t  - **Where the fix goes:** both the tokenizer and `_unquote`.\n450\t  - **What it reads:** every escape form that the pinned SWI version writes,\n451\t    listed at the gate.\n452\t  - **Boundaries:** U+10FFFF (`\\4177777\\`) is accepted. `\\4200000\\`, the\n453\t    surrogate `\\154000\\` and an escape of more than 7 digits refuse with an\n454\t    octal-specific message (p2).\n455\t  - **A token-boundary case** yields two constants.\n456\t- **IL1-C4 (p1):**\n457\t  - **The run of record:** one live Popper run over the IK6 refunds export,\n458\t    under IL-R9.\n459\t  - **Its outcome:** one `termination` value and one `import` value (p2).\n460\t  - **What is retained:** raw stdout, the extracted program, an\n461\t    independent-evaluation run record (IL-R4), the import report or the\n462\t    refusal line, and the versions.\n463\t  - **Replay (p2):** a default-gate test checks four things:\n464\t    - the shared extractor reproduces `program.pl` (or nothing) from the\n465\t      committed raw stdout;\n466\t    - with a program, it re-runs `gnd import ilp-rules` with the recorded\n467\t      fixed arguments and asserts the exit, the path-normalized report and\n468\t      the proposal's SHA-256;\n469\t    - for a refusal, it asserts the refusal line instead;\n470\t    - with no program, it asserts `import: none`.\n471\t- **IL1-C5 (p1):** the importer half of the join space is already closed (IK5\n472\t  case I2). A synthetic fixture on which the stand-in learner yields at least\n473\t  one join candidate gets a case. If no such fixture exists within the stand-in\n474\t  learner's rules, the row stays carried with that reason.\n475\t\n476\t### IL2 — ILASP / FastLAS adapter\n477\t\n478\t- **IL2-C1:** the ASP task equals a committed golden across two hash seeds. The\n479\t  golden pins the background as comparisons only, per-example contexts and the\n480\t  `#modeb` arities.\n481\t- **IL2-C2 (p1):** the ASP reader maps a fixture hypothesis to the clause form.\n482\t  Every refused construct in §2 has one test with its name. The constants table\n483\t  has one test per kind.\n484\t- **IL2-C3:** the run-record binding and the coverage re-check use IK5's code,\n485\t  with `method: ilp-ilasp`.\n486\t- **IL2-C4 (p1):** one live run under IL-R9, with its licence recorded first\n487\t  (IL-R5), and a replay test.\n488\t\n489\t### IL3 — Boolean rule sets\n490\t\n491\t- **IL3-C1:** the feature table equals a committed golden across two hash\n492\t  seeds. The threshold and feature bounds are fixed in the spec before results\n493\t  (IK-R12), and the work counter stays under its ceiling.\n494\t- **IL3-C2 (p1):** each row of the polarity table has a test per learner output\n495\t  spelling. A positive single-feature conjunct maps to a clause that re-checks.\n496\t- **IL3-C3:** the coverage re-check compares the learner's per-conjunct covered\n497\t  populations with GND's, set-wise. Those populations come from the learner's\n498\t  own `predict` (IL-R4).\n499\t- **IL3-C4:** one live run of a named rule-set learner in a scratch Python\n500\t  environment, under IL-R9, with a replay test.\n501\t\n502\t### IL4 — AMIE-style miner\n503\t\n504\t- **IL4-C1:** the typed triple export equals a committed golden across two hash\n505\t  seeds, and skipped fact types are listed.\n506\t- **IL4-C2:** a mined rule maps to a schema-valid `guarded` constraint with an\n507\t  `atom` consequent. Constants, recursion and non-binary atoms are handled as\n508\t  the IL4 spec's table says, each with a test.\n509\t- **IL4-C3 (p1):** GND's numerator, denominator and confidence match a\n510\t  brute-force oracle over distinct head pairs on small fixtures.\n511\t  `support-mismatch`, `covers-nothing`, `open-world-head`, `type-confused-join`\n512\t  (through the shared function) (p2) and the refusal of a rule that does not\n513\t  hold each have a test.\n514\t- **IL4-C5 (p2):** the post-parse `--labels`/`--population` check exits 64.\n515\t  The missing-`--labels` exit for `popper` and `neutral` is unchanged. The two\n516\t  moved RF1 goldens are listed in the IL4 record.\n517\t- **IL4-C4:** one live run under IL-R9, with JVM and miner versions recorded,\n518\t  and a replay test.\n519\t\n520\t### IL5 — LLM proposer\n521\t\n522\t- **IL5-C1 (p1):** `gnd.export.ilp_prompt` is deterministic for fixed inputs (a\n523\t  golden digest) and neutralizes host strings. A hostile description or value\n524\t  fixture cannot change the prompt's instruction section.\n525\t- **IL5-C2:** a malformed agent answer exits 3 at the clauses reader. A\n526\t  well-formed answer